    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Tyler Freeman** (69.19) and **Lawrence Butler** (66.42) anchor our lineup, benefiting from prime park factors and favorable platoon dynamics. **Tyler Freeman** is the day\u2019s strongest play; he gains significant utility from a +14% park adjustment and an advantageous look at Bubba Chandler. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** is poised for a high-ceiling performance against Jos\u00e9 Soriano, buoyed by a +13% platoon edge that makes him an essential start despite a lower-middle-of-the-order projection. We are also monitoring the matchup against Roki Sasaki; while **Jeremiah Jackson** (60.78) and **Taylor Ward** (63.60) both face a tough arm, the combination of wind-out conditions and Sasaki\u2019s slow delivery tempo\u2014which provides an opportunistic boost for our SB-focused strategy\u2014keeps them firmly in the \"Strong\" start tier.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s bench was **Drew Gilbert** (38.45). While he hovered near the marginal threshold, the optimizer ultimately pivoted away from him due to the stiff challenge posed by Lake Bachar, whose 3.40 xERA significantly drags down the efficiency of the entire bottom half of our lineup, including **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Gavin Sheets**. **Masyn Winn** remains a high-floor utility play at 56.74; while he faces an elite holding pitcher in Seth Lugo that limits his SB ceiling, his overall efficiency remains superior to the bench alternatives. As we navigate the \"Lineup Pending\" status for the bulk of our starters, caution is advised for **Yainer Diaz** and **Cal Raleigh**; both are currently projected for moderate scores, but any negative shift in their confirmed batting order\u2014specifically if **Cal Raleigh** slides out of the #2 spot\u2014could force a re-evaluation of their projected efficiency. We continue to manage our roster around the IL absences of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Daulton Varsho**, ensuring we maximize every available plate appearance to maintain our edge in the 5x5 standings.",
